## [upstream] specfunc: report GSL_EDOM for sin_pi and cos_pi at infinity

Commits: 7c27b358b, f87e96143, b4a012ca9

`gsl_sf_cos_pi_e()` reduced its argument with `fabs(intx) >= TWOBIG`
before doing anything else, and infinity satisfies that test, so
`gsl_sf_cos_pi(+-inf)` returned exactly 1.0 with `GSL_SUCCESS` - the
answer for a large *finite* argument, silently given for an argument
that is not in the domain.  Both functions now reject a non-finite
argument with `DOMAIN_ERROR`, so they return `NaN` with `GSL_EDOM`, as
the rest of `specfunc` does for an argument outside its domain.

`GSL_EDOM` rather than the `GSL_ELOSS` of the posted patch: `ELOSS` is
reserved for an in-domain argument whose answer is imprecise, and the
patch's `val = 0.0, err = 1.0` was not even the value `cos_pi` was
reporting.  Across `doc/*.rst`, 48 functions list `GSL_EDOM` alone and
only three list `GSL_ELOSS` alone, all of them pure accuracy cases
(`polar_to_rect`, `angle_restrict_symm`, `angle_restrict_pos`).

NaN is deliberately left alone.  No `specfunc` function treats a NaN
argument as a domain error - NaN propagates through the arithmetic, and
where a convergence check exists it surfaces as `GSL_EMAXITER` instead
(see `gsl_sf_ellint_Kcomp_e`) - so trapping it here would be unlike
anything else in the library.

`gsl_sf_sin_pi` and `gsl_sf_cos_pi` had no documentation at all, which
is why the intended behaviour was ambiguous in the first place.  Both
are now documented with an explicit domain and return value, including
the exact result for arguments beyond `2/epsilon`, which are even
integers as far as a `double` can represent.  Tests cover infinity,
NaN, and large finite arguments.

The only callers inside the library are `gsl_sf_bessel_Jnu_e` and
`gsl_sf_bessel_Yn_e`, which propagate the status with
`GSL_ERROR_SELECT_4`; they can only reach the new guard when the order
`nu` is itself infinite, which was already meaningless.

Files: `specfunc/sincos_pi.c`, `specfunc/test_sincos_pi.c`,
`doc/specfunc-trig.rst`.

See Savannah bug #57978.


## [upstream] specfunc: fix erfc, log_erfc and erf_Z for very large arguments

Commits: 708791c25, 6bc4d8cdd, 9daf0cf36

Three separate overflows let a `NaN` reach the result of the error
function family.  All of them need arguments far outside anything a
user is likely to pass, but they are reachable through the library's own
`gsl_sf_erf_Q_e` and `gsl_sf_hazard_e`, which propagate `erfc`'s value
and error.

`gsl_sf_erfc_e()` for `|x| > 1e62`: `erfc8_sum()` builds its numerator
and denominator by Horner to degree 5 and 6, so both overflow to
infinity and their ratio is `NaN`.  Since `erfc(x)` has already
underflowed to zero by `x = 27.213` - where it reaches the least
denormal, 4.9e-324 - that whole range is now taken directly, which
makes `erfc(+-inf)` exactly 0 and 2.

`gsl_sf_log_erfc_e()` for `x > 2.4e51`: `log_erfc8()` computes
`log(erfc(x)exp(x*x)) - x*x`.  Above that the ratio underflows to zero,
so the log gives `-inf`, and above `1e62` it is `NaN`.  Both now fall
back to the leading term of the asymptotic expansion,
`-x*x - log(x) - log(sqrt(pi))`, which is accurate to a few ulp there.
Beyond `sqrt(DBL_MAX)` the `x*x` overflows as well, the answer has no
representable form, and it is `-inf`.

`gsl_sf_erf_Z_e()` at `x = +-inf`: the error term `fabs(x * val)` is
`inf * 0`.

The error estimates were unusable over the same ranges, for the same
reasons: `x*x` overflowed in the `erfc` error term, and
`2*eps*fabs(val)` overflowed in the `log_erfc` one.

A `NaN` argument is still left to propagate, as it is everywhere else in
`specfunc`; the two posted patches both guard with `!isnan(x)`, which
would silently turn a `NaN` input into a plausible finite answer.

Neither posted patch is taken as it stands.  `#53919` is the closer of
the two but only patches the symptom.  `#65760` abandons
`log_erfc8()` above `x = 100` - where it is still perfectly accurate -
in favour of the same asymptotic formula, with an error estimate of
exactly 0.0, and does not cover the overflow its own comment describes.

The three functions were documented only as `Exceptional Return Values:
none`, with no domain line, which is what let the two reports disagree
about the same input.  All three are defined for all real `x`, since
each has a finite limit at infinity (`erf` to `+-1`, `erfc` to 0 and 2,
`log(erfc)` to `log 2` and `-inf`), and the docs now say so; this is
`GSL_SUCCESS`, not a domain error, in contrast to `sin_pi`/`cos_pi`
above, which have no limit at infinity.

Files: `specfunc/erfc.c`, `specfunc/test_sf.c`,
`doc/specfunc-erf.rst`.

See Savannah bugs #53919 and #65760.

Related, not done: `gsl_sf_hazard_e()` takes its `x >= 25` branch for
`|x|` beyond `sqrt(DBL_MAX)`, where `1/(x*x)` underflows and the result is
